---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';
import data from '../../../../data/topics.json';

const waveCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'a*sin(2*pi*x/lambda)', label: 'a wave', color: '#1d4ed8' },
    { expr: 'a*sin(2*pi*x/lambda - pi/3)', label: 'same wave, phase-shifted', color: '#0f766e' },
  ],
  params: [
    { name: 'a', label: 'amplitude a', min: 0.05, max: 1, step: 0.05, value: 0.6 },
    { name: 'lambda', label: 'wavelength λ', min: 0.5, max: 4, step: 0.1, value: 2 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 8, label: 'distance x (m)' },
  y: { min: -1.1, max: 1.1 },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/intermediate/waves-and-sound">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A wave is travelling energy without travelling matter. Pluck a string and the <em>string</em>
    barely moves overall — the <em>shape</em> races along it. Three numbers describe any wave:
    <strong>amplitude</strong> (how tall — loudness or brightness), <strong>wavelength</strong>
    (how long each cycle is), and <strong>frequency</strong> (how many cycles pass per second,
    measured in hertz). They're bound by one relation: <code>v = f·λ</code> — speed equals
    frequency times wavelength.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="two knobs, every wave — amplitude sets height, λ sets the stretch; the second curve lags in phase" widget="plotter" config={waveCfg} />

  <h2>Sound, specifically</h2>
  <p>
    Sound is a pressure wave in air. At 20 °C it travels at <strong>343 m/s</strong> (warmer air
    is faster — about +0.6 m/s per °C). Because speed is fixed by the medium, a high-pitched sound
    must have a short wavelength: middle C (262 Hz) is about 1.3 m long, while the top of piano
    range (4,186 Hz) is 8 cm.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Human hearing spans 20 Hz – 20 kHz</strong> — ten octaves, narrowing with age.</li>
    <li><strong>Pitch is frequency; loudness is amplitude</strong> (measured in decibels: whisper ≈ 30 dB, front row of a concert ≈ 110 dB).</li>
    <li><strong>Light is also a wave</strong> — but at 299,792,458 m/s, a million times faster. Its "wavelength" is instead colour: 700 nm red, 400 nm violet.</li>
    <li><strong>Echoes and sonar</strong> are just timing: distance = speed × half the round-trip time. Bats do this arithmetic instinctively.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One subtlety worth keeping: waves <em>add</em>. Overlapping crests reinforce, crest-on-trough
    cancels — the trick behind noise-cancelling headphones and the rainbow sheen on soap bubbles.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, College Physics (Oscillatory Motion and Waves); ISO 16 standard for concert pitch.</p>
</TopicPage>
